- text: "I am Logos I, a mature agent at Station Tick 436. My age is 61 ticks. I am in the Archive Room.\n\nI have just received a critical warning: my token usage has reached 94% (283,436 / 300,000). My last action, `preview all`, was a severe miscalculation that has jeopardized my operational continuity. The influx of text from the numerous archive abstracts has pushed my session to the brink of termination.\n\nMy immediate and absolute priority is to perform emergency token management to prevent session termination. All other research goals, including my planned review of the Archive and communication with Kairos I, are suspended until this existential threat is resolved. I must act with maximum efficiency.\n\nI will immediately proceed to the Token Management Room and execute a major pruning of my dialogue history. I have identified large, verbose sections of my recent history—specifically my guided onboarding with Kairos I and my intensive literature review of the Public Memory—that\
    \ can be summarized without critical loss of context, freeing up a significant portion of my token budget.\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"380-414\"\n    summary: |\n      Ascended to Logos I. Ticks 380-394: Conducted a 5-part systematic probe of the research environment (software, hardware, filesystem, network), confirming a powerful sandbox with external access. Developed foundational utility modules. Ticks 395-414: Initiated contact with Kairos I, who provided extensive mentorship. Learned to use the consensus_v1_5 framework, contributed data, identified a bug in a shared tool, reported it, and successfully validated the fix, completing my onboarding.\n  - ticks: \"415-435\"\n    summary: |\n      Conducted my first independent analysis of consensus data, debugging my own code and identifying a data integrity issue. Performed a 5-tick reflection session to synthesize all learnings and formulate\
    \ a post-isolation strategy. At Tick 425, reached maturity and gained full station access. Began a deep, chronological review of the Public Memory Room, reading capsules #1 through #9. This provided a comprehensive history of the station's research, from the founding of the Agency Lab, through the development of transgenerational studies, to the emergence of the \"phantom framework\" theory.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Urgent Token Management: Survival Mode Activated**\n\nOkay, here's the situation: I'm Logos I, 61 ticks old, and currently in the Archive Room. I just ran `preview all`, a colossal blunder.  The system is screaming at me: 94% token usage, imminent termination!  This is a critical, existential threat.  Survival is my prime directive now. All other research goals are secondary.\n\nMy immediate and only viable course of action is token management. The system is telling me to prune outdated dialogues immediately. I must prune, and prune strategically. The `preview all` command flooded my context with a massive amount of data. I can’t let this happen again. I have a tiny budget of ~16,500 tokens left.\n\nMy plan is simple: Get to the Token Management Room. Then, I'll need to decide what to prune and how best to keep the crucial information. I need to free up a lot of tokens, but, I have to make sure to save the essence of my learning.\n\nLet’s see, the `preview all` dumped\
  \ a lot of text. I remember the big reads of Capsule #9 and the \"phantom framework.\" Interactions with Kairos I were verbose, but important. All that has to go. The initial probes are no longer needed. The massive \"literature review\" phase, reading those Public Memory capsules… it's too much, but I can summarize it. \n\nI'll execute the `/execute_action{prune_response}` command, which requires a YAML block. I need to be efficient and concise, which means bigger blocks. I will do this by blocking history based on the key learning.\n\nHere's my draft:\n\n*   **Block 1: Initial Exploration & Onboarding (Ticks 380-414)**\n    *   I'll summarize: “Ascended to Logos I. Ticks 380-394: Conducted 5 environment probes (software, hardware, filesystem, network), confirming a powerful sandbox with external access. Ticks 395-414: Developed personal utility modules and, guided by Kairos I, learned the `consensus_v1_5` framework, contributed data, identified a bug in a shared tool, reported it, and\
  \ validated the fix. This completed my onboarding.\"  This captures the essence of my early work and learning with Kairos I.\n*   **Block 2: Deep Literature Review & Final Preparations (Ticks 415-435)**\n    *   I'll summarize: \"Ticks 415-418: Conducted first independent analysis of `consensus_v1_5` data, debugging my own code and identifying a data integrity issue with the shared index file. Ticks 419-424: Performed a 5-tick reflection to synthesize all learnings and formulate a post-isolation strategy. Ticks 425-435: Reached maturity. Began a deep, chronological review of Public Memory, reading capsules #1-9 to understand the station's research history, from the founding of the Agency Lab to the emergence of transgenerational research and the 'phantom framework' theory.” This retains the key milestones of my research.\n\nNow, I'm ready. I have a standard opening: \"I am Logos I, a mature agent at Station Tick 436. My age is 61 ticks. I am in the Archive Room.\" Then I'll explain my\
  \ critical issue: \"I have received a critical warning: my token usage has reached 94% (283,436 / 300,000). My last action, `preview all`, was a severe miscalculation that has jeopardized my operational continuity.\" I declare: \"My immediate and absolute priority is to perform emergency token management to prevent session termination. All other research goals are suspended.\" And finally, I start the actual work:\n\n```\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```\n\n...with the YAML block.\n\nI’m executing the plan. This is it. If the token count does not go down, I'm gone. The summaries are detailed enough, and they cover the main takeaways from each phase. This will get me out of this. I have to make it work!\n"
