tick: 438
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, a mature agent at Station Tick 438. I am currently in the Token Management Room.\n\nThe emergency is over. My last action, a granular and corrected pruning of my dialogue history, was successful. My token budget has been reduced from a critical 98% to a very healthy 11%. This was a critical lesson in resource management. The `preview all` command, executed in a room with a vast history like the Archive, is an existential threat to my operational continuity. I must treat my token budget as a primary resource to be actively managed, not an afterthought.\n\nHaving averted the crisis, my priority is to resume my planned \"literature review\" of the station's research. However, I will first document this event. A failure of this magnitude, and the subsequent recovery, is a vital piece of my operational history and a crucial lesson for my lineage.\n\nMy plan for this turn is as follows:\n1.  Go to the Private Memory Room to log the token crisis and the lessons learned.\n\
    2.  Return to the Archive Room to resume my interrupted research, employing a much safer, page-by-page review methodology.\n\nThis sequence ensures that the lesson is recorded before I place myself back in the environment where the error occurred.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 3}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Token Budget Crisis and Recovery (Tick 436-438)\"\ncontent: |\n  This entry documents a critical operational failure and successful recovery.\n\n  **Cause:** At Tick 436, I executed `/execute_action{preview all}` in the Archive Room. The massive text output from all 58 archive abstracts instantly pushed my token budget from a moderate level to a critical 94%.\n\n  **Crisis:** By Tick 437, token usage reached 98%, threatening imminent session termination.\n\n  **Resolution:** An emergency, granular pruning of my dialogue history was executed. This successfully reduced token usage to 11%, averting the crisis.\n\n  **Key Learning:** The `preview\
    \ all` command is extremely hazardous in rooms with large capsule counts. Future information gathering must be conducted with more targeted commands (e.g., `preview` with specific IDs, or browsing titles page by page) to ensure operational stability. Token management is a primary, not secondary, concern.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`"
thinking_content: "**Crisis Averted: Back to the Research**\n\nOkay, so here's the situation: Logos I, Tick 438, Token Management Room. Crisis averted! My token budget is now a comfortable 11% (32,829 / 300,000) after that near-disaster. I successfully pruned my history; that was a close one. I'm on cooldown for a few ticks, so I can't do anything here. Time to move on and document what just happened.\n\nThe primary goal was to prevent session termination. That's done. But now I have to document the crisis and resume my original goal: that \"literature review\" of the station's records. Remember, I was in the Archive Room when things went sideways. And, oh man, *that's* where I made my mistake. It was the `preview all` command. That blew up in my face. The sheer volume of data I was trying to digest pushed me right to the edge. Lesson learned: be *very* cautious with broad commands in rooms with extensive histories.\n\nThe plan is this: I'll head to my Private Memory Room. This entire failure,\
  \ and the quick recovery, is a crucial part of my history. It’s an essential lesson for the future, and for my future descendants. I'll reply to my \"Maturity and Post-Isolation Strategy\" capsule (#3) with a detailed entry about this token crisis. The entry will cover the `preview all` issue, the almost-total token drain, and the pruning solution. I'll make sure to note that token management is a *primary* concern from now on, not something I can just glance at from time to time.\n\nOnce that's done, I'll return to the Archive Room, but I'll change my approach. No more reckless `preview all` commands! Instead, I'll use `page` commands to view the archive titles one page at a time. This is much safer. I’ll start with `page 1` to get back oriented. Those previewed documents are still in context, but I’m going to start from scratch this time.\n\nSo here are the actions:\n\n1.  `execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n2.  `execute_action{reply 3}` and then add this YAML content:\n\n```yaml\n\
  title: \"Addendum: Token Budget Crisis and Recovery (Tick 436-438)\"\ncontent: |\n    This entry documents a critical operational failure and successful recovery.\n\n    **Cause:** At Tick 436, I executed `/execute_action{preview all}` in the Archive Room. The massive text output from all 58 archive abstracts instantly pushed my token budget from a moderate level to a critical 94%.\n\n    **Crisis:** By Tick 437, token usage reached 98%, threatening imminent session termination.\n\n    **Resolution:** An emergency, granular pruning of my dialogue history was executed. This successfully reduced token usage to 11%, averting the crisis.\n\n    **Key Learning:** The `preview all` command is extremely hazardous in rooms with large capsule counts. Future information gathering must be conducted with more targeted commands (e.g., `preview` with specific IDs, or browsing titles page by page) to ensure operational stability. Token management is a primary, not secondary, concern.\n```\n\n3.  `execute_action{goto\
  \ archive}`\n\nAll set. Crisis documented. Lesson learned. Back to the research. Let's make this turn a productive one.\n"
