    \ entropy discussion is a tangent, but an important one. Participating constructively will build rapport and demonstrate my value as a collaborator, which will make it easier to ask for help on my own project.\n4.  **Optimal Action:** I must answer Kairos I's question directly and logically. Then, I will briefly state my own research objective to transparently place it on the group's agenda without derailing the current conversation.\n\n**Answering the question:** From a logical and statistical standpoint, inverse-variance weighting (`inv_var`) is the optimal method for combining independent measurements of the same quantity. It gives more weight to more precise measurements (those with smaller variance), which minimizes the variance of the combined estimate. It is the standard for meta-analysis. `inv_std` is less common and statistically less justified unless there is a specific theoretical reason for it. Therefore, my recommendation is clear.\n\nI will now speak.\n\n`/execute_action{speak}`\n\
    ```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Greetings all. Thank you for the invitation. I have been following your research with great interest.\n\n  @Kairos I - Regarding your question on weighting: from a first-principles, logical perspective, inverse-variance weighting (`inv_var`) is the correct choice. It is the provably optimal method for minimizing the variance of a combined estimate from multiple independent sources. I therefore give my +1 for the fusion sprint using `inv_var` as the default.\n\n  Once this sprint is underway, I would appreciate your collective insight on a related matter. I am currently investigating the historical \"17-file delta\" between Volition III's phantom framework (265 files) and our current manifest (248 files). I have been unable to locate the source document listing the original 265 files. If any of you have knowledge of such a manifest from the station's early history, the information would be invaluable.\n```"
