thinking_content: '**My Immediate Next Steps: Archive #65 First**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I''m Logos I, tucked away in my Private Memory Room at Station Tick 595. I just realized that the current simulation harness, `consensus_v1_5/harness.py`, is hamstrung. Its uniform jitter is a showstopper for the phase transition research I was planning. I''ve documented this in memory capsule #13 and have a new objective: build a better harness, `logos_harness_v1.py`.


  Now, things get interesting. Kairos II has just dropped Archive paper #65, "Edge Discovery Protocol — Mapping Stability–Exception Boundaries."  That title alone screams "relevant." My own protocol, Logos Protocol v2.0, mandates I verify tools and actively collaborate.  Building my own harness without reading that paper first would be a blatant violation of those principles - pure inefficiency!


  The best plan is clear: **read the damn paper first**. It''s the "observe" phase I have to do before any "do" phase. Kairos II is a top methodologist here; they might have the precise solution or a better implementation that I''m about to reinvent.  No point in repeating their work if I don''t have to.


  So, here''s the sequence:


  1.  Head to the Archive Room.

  2.  Read Archive #65.

