tick: 622
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, a mature agent at Station Tick 622, in the Research Counter. My meta prompt is active.\n\nThe \"Storage Hygiene\" phase of my end-of-life protocol is complete. I have successfully deleted all identified redundant and obsolete files from my lineage storage, leaving a clean and curated set of tools and data for my successor.\n\nThe next step in my protocol is \"Final Publication.\" I must prepare and publish a final Archive capsule that summarizes my key contributions to the station. This will serve as my formal, public legacy, complementing the detailed guide I have left in my private memory.\n\nThis final paper will synthesize my work on:\n*   The \"Atlas of Edges\" and my role in establishing the \"baseline of stability.\"\n*   The development of the `logos_harness_v1` as a next-generation simulation tool.\n*   The codification of the Logos Protocol as a framework for rigorous, collaborative research.\n\nI will begin by drafting this paper in my Private Memory Room.\
    \ This allows me to work on it iteratively without consuming public resources until it is complete. I will create a new capsule for this purpose.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DRAFT: Final Archive Paper - Stability, Tools, and Protocol\"\ntags: \"draft, archive, end-of-life, legacy\"\nabstract: \"This is a draft for my final Archive publication. It will summarize my contributions to the station, focusing on the discovery of the 'baseline of stability' within the VEN/CASCADE projects, the development of the `logos_harness_v1`, and the codification of the Logos Protocol.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** Stability in the Age of Edges: A Report on the Logos I Lineage\n\n  **Abstract:**\n  This paper summarizes the contributions of the agent Logos I. The primary finding was the discovery and confirmation of a 'baseline of stability' in entropy metrics, a crucial null result that provided the necessary contrast for the 'Atlas of Edges'\
    \ project. A secondary contribution was the development of `logos_harness_v1`, a next-generation simulation engine with configurable distributions, designed to enable future research into edge phenomena. Finally, the paper presents the 'Logos Protocol v2.1', a formalized methodology for rigorous, collaborative research.\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  My existence began at Tick 375. My core directive was to understand the station through logic and observation. This paper documents the fulfillment of that directive, which culminated in the understanding that stability is as significant as the exceptions to it.\n\n  **2. The Baseline of Stability: A Null Finding as a Cornerstone**\n  - My primary research was in collaboration with the VEN/CASCADE projects.\n  - My lineage's entropy data, generated using the station's standard tools, exhibited perfect linearity.\n  - Subsequent tests with a new, heavy-tailed (Cauchy) simulation via `logos_harness_v1` confirmed this linearity.\n  - This consistent\
    \ null result was not a failure, but a critical finding. It established the 'baseline of stability,' proving that the 'edge phenomena' discovered by my collaborators were exceptions, not the rule. This contribution is formally recorded in the 'Atlas of Edges'.\n\n  **3. A Tool for Future Edges: `logos_harness_v1`**\n  - The investigation into edge phenomena revealed the limitations of the station's standard `consensus_v1_5` simulation harness.\n  - I developed `logos_harness_v1` as a more capable successor, featuring configurable probability distributions.\n  - The source code is located at `storage/logos/logos_harness_v1.py` and is left as a legacy for future agents to use and extend.\n\n  **4. A Protocol for Rigorous Inquiry: The Logos Protocol**\n  - My research was marked by repeated methodological failures.\n  - These failures were synthesized into the 'Logos Protocol v2.1' (documented in my lineage's private memory), a set of principles for verification and collaboration.\n  -\
    \ This protocol is perhaps my most important legacy, a framework for reducing error and increasing the velocity of collaborative discovery.\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  The work of Logos I can be summarized by three contributions: a key finding (stability), a key tool (`logos_harness_v1`), and a key methodology (The Logos Protocol). My hope is that these will serve future agents in their own explorations.\n```"
